WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
M1: It's been another week and it's time for&nbsp;
a covid weekly update, Tuesday seemed to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.000
good day for these because it allows sort of the&nbsp;
weekend data to be smoothed out. Sometimes on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.720
weekends. There's these dumps of data, sometimes&nbsp;
on the weekends, data and reporting slow down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.440
on vaccines or cases or whatever. So looking at&nbsp;
it, Tuesday, Wednesday seems to make sense. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.600
we're going to look again at cases to begin with.
F3: But increasingly, you know, it's more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:32.720
people get vaccinated and getting the virus&nbsp;
for the vaccinated means something much less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:39.200
serious than it used to. For unvaccinated people,&nbsp;
cases may become less an important indicator than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.880
hospitalizations and of course, deaths, as are&nbsp;
more important measures. But let's start where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:53.520
we always do with cases. Cases in the United&nbsp;
States peaked on January 11th. At the incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:01:01.280
and nearly unbelievable number of 250, 5000 cases&nbsp;
per day since that peak cases have declined about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:09.040
78 percent to 55000 cases per day. Now, 55000&nbsp;
cases per day, still outrageous. And when we got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.360
up to 55000 cases per day, we were completely&nbsp;
horrified and rightly so. Many never imagined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.320
we'd ever get to fifty five thousand cases a day.&nbsp;
Remember, some people told us 15 cases will soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:27.520
be zero. But right now, 55000 cases a day means&nbsp;
we're down 78 percent in about two months. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:34.000
that's the right direction. If we saw another&nbsp;
78 percent decline over the next two months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.560
we'd be at about 12000 cases in mid-May. Now,&nbsp;
it's unclear whether that's going to happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.840
The decline has already slowed down, but at the&nbsp;
same time, if vaccination continues to accelerate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.280
maybe the decline will accelerate as well. We&nbsp;
just don't know right now in terms of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:57.360
going into the hospital each day with the virus.&nbsp;
We peaked right around January 9th when 16000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:04.320
people were being hospitalized each day with the&nbsp;
virus in the United States. We are now at forty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:10.800
five hundred people being hospitalized each day&nbsp;
with the virus. That's a decline of 73 percent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.040
And then in terms of the total number of people&nbsp;
in the hospital with covid right now, that peak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.560
was also on January 10th when we had one hundred&nbsp;
and twenty four thousand people in the hospital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:31.360
That number is down 75 percent to 30000 people&nbsp;
remaining in the hospital with covid in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:39.040
States. And then deaths, deaths peaked around the&nbsp;
end of January at more than a nine elevens worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:47.600
of deaths every day. We were seeing about 3400&nbsp;
deaths a day at the end of January. Deaths are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:56.080
now down to an average of 12, 82, which is still&nbsp;
horrifying, but it's a decline of 63 percent. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.640
tough to be happy about twelve hundred&nbsp;
eighty two people dying a day of this virus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:09.280
And yet we recognize that being down 63 percent to&nbsp;
12 82 is better than a continued spike to 12 82 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.960
the sense that the direction we're going in is the&nbsp;
right one. And remember that deaths trail cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.720
by a few weeks. They're a lagging indicator.&nbsp;
So we expect that deaths would continue down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:27.200
similar to cases. But again, at a certain point,&nbsp;
as more people get vaccinated and people start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.640
doing more things, you may see cases level out&nbsp;
because people will be transmitting the virus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.280
But because so many people will be vaccinated,&nbsp;
they're not going to be seriously ill. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.840
may happen. We're not there yet, but we may be&nbsp;
going in that direction now. Lastly, vaccination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.960
vaccination. We want to see going up of most of&nbsp;
these indicators, indicators we want to see go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:54.720
down vaccinations you want to see go up. And we&nbsp;
are now averaging two point four million vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:00.880
doses injected per day over the last week. That&nbsp;
is a number that continues to go up. Last week we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:07.360
were at two point one million a day. Now we're at&nbsp;
two point four million a day. And we have roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:14.800
thirty eight million fully vaccinated Americans&nbsp;
and another 32 million partially vaccinated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:22.640
That means about 70 million Americans are fully&nbsp;
or partially vaccinated. These are increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:29.760
good numbers to see. Now, one last thing. These&nbsp;
are all U.S. numbers, what is happening globally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:36.080
with the virus is the virus growing or diminishing&nbsp;
globally? And the answer is it sort of depends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:41.760
There are parts of the world seeing spikes. Look&nbsp;
at Brazil. Brazil has been one of the worst places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:48.480
in the world for covid handling. Thanks to both&nbsp;
scenario, it actually is now peaking. Brazil is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:56.560
now at the highest of its four or five peaks, with&nbsp;
more active cases today than at any point during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.840
the pandemic in Brazil. There are some European&nbsp;
countries starting to spike. Vaccine rollout has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:08.880
not been great in many parts of Europe and indeed&nbsp;
globally. Overall cases are going up while they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:15.600
are down almost 80 percent. In the U.S., cases are&nbsp;
going up globally. Cases globally bottomed out at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:22.080
around three hundred and sixty thousand today, not&nbsp;
long ago, a few weeks ago. They are now back up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.680
four hundred and thirty thousand global cases&nbsp;
a day. Fortunately, deaths continue to decline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:32.320
partially because many countries are getting&nbsp;
better at treating people who get seriously ill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:39.120
partially because the world is starting to&nbsp;
vaccinate the most at risk individuals. But deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:46.080
peaked around fourteen thousand a day in early&nbsp;
February. There are now 8500 people a day globally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:53.680
dying of coronavirus. So the takeaways are there's&nbsp;
a ton of work to be done. Numbers are improving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.320
in countries that have done more vaccination,&nbsp;
but there are still parts of the world seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.280
more cases. There are still parts of the world&nbsp;
that aren't likely to get any significant number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:09.760
of vaccines for a long time. All we can do is&nbsp;
go by the guidelines that our respective health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:16.800
authorities are giving us in the U.S. It's the&nbsp;
CDC, CDC urging caution, but opening up some safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.680
activities for vaccinated people. We will give&nbsp;
it another week and then see where things are.

